(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "9.2", title: "Algorithms and Cambridge pseudocode", q: "How do we judge whether one algorithm is better than another?", lessons: [
{ t: "Why pseudocode?", o: "I can explain what pseudocode is and compare it with Python.",
  k: ["pseudocode: a structured way of writing an algorithm that is not tied to one programming language", "keyword: a word with a fixed meaning, written in capitals in Cambridge pseudocode", "algorithm: a set of steps that solves a problem"],
  d: ["A Python program and a flowchart can describe the same algorithm. Name one advantage of each.", "Python can be run; a flowchart shows the route at a glance."],
  s: ["What pseudocode is|Structured English for writing algorithms|Not a real language, but with agreed rules|Cambridge exam papers use one standard style",
      "Side by side|Python: `total = total + mark`|Pseudocode: `Total ← Total + Mark`|Python: `print(total)`  Pseudocode: `OUTPUT Total`",
      "Why use it?|Anyone can read it, whatever language they program in|You think about the logic, not the punctuation|In CS Toolkit the Pseudocode runner can run it and build a trace table"],
  a: "Match ten Python lines to their pseudocode equivalents, then open the Pseudocode runner in CS Toolkit, load the 'Totalling and counting' example and run it. Support: use the keyword card. Stretch: rewrite the example's FOR loop as a WHILE loop in pseudocode and check it gives the same output.",
  w: [["What is pseudocode?", "A structured way of writing an algorithm that is not tied to one programming language."], ["Write `print('Hello')` in Cambridge pseudocode.", "OUTPUT \"Hello\""], ["Which symbol is used for assignment in Cambridge pseudocode?", "← (an arrow)."], ["How are keywords written in Cambridge pseudocode?", "In capital letters."], ["Explain why exam boards use pseudocode instead of one programming language.", "Students learn different languages, and pseudocode lets everyone read and write the same algorithm while focusing on the logic."]],
  x: ["Translate into pseudocode: `count = count + 1`", "Count ← Count + 1"] },
{ t: "DECLARE, assignment, INPUT and OUTPUT", o: "I can declare variables and write input, assignment and output in pseudocode.",
  k: ["DECLARE: the keyword that names a variable and its data type", "data type: INTEGER, REAL, CHAR, STRING or BOOLEAN", "CONSTANT: a named value that cannot change while the program runs"],
  d: ["Which Python data types match INTEGER, REAL and STRING?", "int, float and str."],
  s: ["Declaring|>> DECLARE Name : STRING|>> DECLARE Age : INTEGER|>> DECLARE Price : REAL|>> CONSTANT VAT = 0.2",
      "Input and output|>> INPUT Name|>> OUTPUT \"Hello \", Name|Commas join items in an OUTPUT|INPUT needs no prompt in pseudocode",
      "Assignment|>> Total ← Price * (1 + VAT)|The arrow means 'becomes'|In the Pseudocode runner, typing <- gives ←"],
  a: "Write pseudocode for four short programs (greeting, area of a rectangle, price with VAT, minutes to hours and minutes using DIV and MOD) and run each in the Pseudocode runner. Support: complete the programs that have the DECLARE lines written. Stretch: tick 'Every variable must be declared' and fix any errors it reports.",
  w: [["Write a declaration for a variable `Score` that holds whole numbers.", "DECLARE Score : INTEGER"], ["Which data type stores TRUE or FALSE?", "BOOLEAN."], ["Write pseudocode to input a length and output it doubled.", "INPUT Length\nOUTPUT Length * 2"], ["What is `17 DIV 5` and what is `17 MOD 5`?", "3 and 2."], ["Explain the difference between a variable and a constant.", "A variable's value can change while the program runs. A constant's value is fixed."]],
  x: ["Which data type would you use for a price such as 4.99?", "REAL."] },
{ t: "Lab: translate Python programs into pseudocode", o: "I can translate working Python into Cambridge pseudocode and run it.",
  k: ["translate: to write the same algorithm in a different form", "syntax: the rules for writing a language", "equivalent: doing exactly the same thing"],
  d: ["Translate: `if age >= 18:`", "IF Age >= 18 THEN"],
  s: ["Method|Read the Python one line at a time|Write the matching pseudocode|Keep the same variable names, with a capital first letter",
      "Run it|Paste into the Pseudocode runner|Read any error: it names the line|Compare the output with the Python version",
      "Common slips|`=` for assignment instead of ←|Missing ENDIF or NEXT|Keywords typed in lower case"],
  a: "Translate the five Python programs on the sheet into pseudocode and run each in the runner with the same inputs as the Python. Record whether the outputs match. Support: translate programs 1 to 3. Stretch: program 5 has a Python feature with no direct pseudocode equivalent. Find a way to express it.",
  w: [["Translate: `name = input('Name: ')`", "INPUT Name"], ["Translate: `total = 0`", "Total ← 0"], ["What must close every IF in pseudocode?", "ENDIF"], ["The runner reports 'Use ← to assign a value'. What is wrong?", "= was used for assignment instead of ←."], ["Explain how you checked that your translation was equivalent.", "I ran both versions with the same inputs and compared the outputs."]],
  x: ["How many of your five translations ran first time?", "A number."] },
{ t: "IF and CASE", o: "I can write selection in pseudocode using IF and CASE.",
  k: ["IF: selection that tests a condition", "CASE: selection that chooses one of several options based on a single value", "OTHERWISE: the CASE option used when no other option matches"],
  d: ["A menu has options 1 to 4. Which is neater: four IF statements or one CASE?", "One CASE, because one value is compared with several options."],
  s: ["IF|>> IF Mark >= 50|>>   THEN|>>     OUTPUT \"Pass\"|>>   ELSE|>>     OUTPUT \"Fail\"|>> ENDIF",
      "Nested IF|An IF inside the THEN or ELSE part|Each IF has its own ENDIF|Indent to show the structure",
      "CASE|>> CASE OF Day|>>   1 : OUTPUT \"Mon\"|>>   2 : OUTPUT \"Tue\"|>>   OTHERWISE : OUTPUT \"Other\"|>> ENDCASE"],
  a: "Write a grade program with nested IF and a menu program with CASE, then run both in the Pseudocode runner. Support: complete the CASE menu with two options missing. Stretch: use a range in CASE, such as 80 TO 100 : OUTPUT \"A\".",
  w: [["Which keyword ends an IF statement?", "ENDIF"], ["Which keyword ends a CASE statement?", "ENDCASE"], ["When does the OTHERWISE option run?", "When the value matches none of the other options."], ["`Day ← 2`. What does the CASE example output?", "Tue"], ["Explain when CASE is better than IF.", "When one variable is compared with several separate values: it is shorter and easier to read."]],
  x: ["Write the first line of a CASE that tests the variable Choice.", "CASE OF Choice"] },
{ t: "FOR, WHILE and REPEAT loops", o: "I can choose and write the three kinds of loop in pseudocode.",
  k: ["count-controlled loop: a FOR loop that repeats a set number of times", "pre-condition loop: a WHILE loop, which tests its condition before each pass", "post-condition loop: a REPEAT loop, which tests its condition after each pass"],
  d: ["Python has for and while. What might a loop that always runs at least once be useful for?", "Asking for input, which must happen at least once before it can be checked."],
  s: ["FOR|>> FOR Count ← 1 TO 10|>>   OUTPUT Count|>> NEXT Count|Use when the number of repeats is known",
      "WHILE|>> WHILE Number <> 0 DO|>>   INPUT Number|>> ENDWHILE|May run zero times",
      "REPEAT|>> REPEAT|>>   INPUT Num|>> UNTIL Num >= 1 AND Num <= 10|Always runs at least once: ideal for validation"],
  a: "Write the same countdown with all three loops and run each. Then choose the best loop for six situations and justify your choice. Support: complete the FOR and WHILE versions. Stretch: use FOR with STEP to output the even numbers from 20 down to 2.",
  w: [["Which loop always runs at least once?", "REPEAT ... UNTIL."], ["Which loop should be used to output the numbers 1 to 100?", "FOR."], ["A REPEAT loop asks for a number until it is from 1 to 10. The inputs are 0, 15, 7. How many times does it ask?", "3."], ["Write a FOR loop that outputs 5, 10, 15, 20.", "FOR N ← 5 TO 20 STEP 5\n  OUTPUT N\nNEXT N"], ["Explain the difference between a pre-condition and a post-condition loop.", "A pre-condition loop (WHILE) tests before each pass, so it may not run at all. A post-condition loop (REPEAT) tests after each pass, so it runs at least once."]],
  x: ["Which loop would you use to validate input, and why?", "REPEAT, because the input must be taken at least once before it can be checked."] },
{ t: "Lab: totalling, counting, maximum and minimum", o: "I can write the standard methods for totalling, counting and finding the largest and smallest.",
  k: ["totalling: adding values to a running total", "counting: adding one to a counter each time something happens", "standard method: a well-known algorithm expected in the exam"],
  d: ["Why is Total set to 0 before a totalling loop?", "So the total starts from nothing."],
  s: ["Totalling and counting|>> Total ← 0|>> Count ← 0|>> FOR I ← 1 TO 10|>>   INPUT Mark|>>   Total ← Total + Mark|>>   IF Mark >= 50 THEN Count ← Count + 1 ENDIF|>> NEXT I",
      "Maximum and minimum|Set Largest and Smallest to the first value|Compare each new value|Replace when bigger or smaller",
      "Average|Calculate after the loop|>> Average ← Total / 10|Use ROUND(Average, 1) to tidy it"],
  a: "Write pseudocode that inputs ten marks and outputs the total, average, how many passed, and the largest and smallest. Run it in the runner with the test data and check against hand calculation. Support: load the 'Maximum and minimum' example and extend it. Stretch: output how many marks were above the average (you will need to store them).",
  w: [["Write the two lines that set up a total and a counter.", "Total ← 0\nCount ← 0"], ["Where is the average calculated: inside or after the loop?", "After the loop."], ["Why set Largest to the first value and not to 0?", "If all values were negative, 0 would wrongly be reported as the largest."], ["Trace: Total ← 0, then FOR N ← 1 TO 4, adding N only when N MOD 2 = 0. Final Total?", "6 (2 + 4)."], ["Explain why finding the number of marks above the average needs the marks to be stored.", "The average is only known after all marks are input, so each mark must be compared with it afterwards."]],
  x: ["Name the four standard methods practised today.", "Totalling, counting, finding the maximum, finding the minimum (accept average)."] },
{ t: "Arrays in pseudocode", o: "I can declare and use one-dimensional and two-dimensional arrays.",
  k: ["array: a fixed-size data structure holding values of one type, accessed by index", "one-dimensional array: a single row of values, using one index", "two-dimensional array: a table of values, using a row and a column index"],
  d: ["In Python the first list item is at index 0. Where does a Cambridge array usually start?", "At index 1, because the bounds are set in the declaration, such as ARRAY[1:5]."],
  s: ["One dimension|>> DECLARE Num : ARRAY[1:5] OF INTEGER|>> Num[1] ← 4|>> OUTPUT Num[1]|The bounds 1:5 give five elements",
      "Looping through|>> Total ← 0|>> FOR I ← 1 TO 5|>>   Total ← Total + Num[I]|>> NEXT I",
      "Two dimensions|>> DECLARE Grid : ARRAY[1:3, 1:4] OF INTEGER|>> Grid[2, 3] ← 7|Row first, then column|Nested FOR loops visit every element"],
  a: "Declare an array of five integers, fill it, then output the total and the largest. Load the '2D array' example in the runner, step through it and watch the Variables panel. Support: complete the totalling loop. Stretch: write nested loops that output the total of each row of a 3 by 4 array.",
  w: [["Write the declaration for an array `Names` of 10 strings indexed from 1.", "DECLARE Names : ARRAY[1:10] OF STRING"], ["Num holds 4, 9, 2, 7, 5. What is the total?", "27."], ["What is the difference between a one-dimensional and a two-dimensional array?", "One uses a single index (a row). Two uses a row and a column index (a table)."], ["In `Grid[2, 3]`, which number is the row?", "2."], ["Explain why an array must be declared with its bounds.", "An array has a fixed size, so the computer must know how many elements to set aside."]],
  x: ["How many elements does ARRAY[1:3, 1:4] hold?", "12."] },
{ t: "Linear search", o: "I can explain and write a linear search.",
  k: ["linear search: checking each item in turn until the target is found or the list ends", "target: the value being searched for", "comparison: checking one item against the target"],
  d: ["Find the card with 4 on it in a face-down row of six cards. What is your method?", "Turn them over one at a time from the start until you find it."],
  s: ["The method|Start at the first item|Compare it with the target|Stop if found, otherwise move to the next|Stop at the end of the list",
      "In pseudocode|>> Found ← FALSE|>> Index ← 1|>> WHILE Index <= 6 AND NOT Found|>>   IF List[Index] = Target THEN Found ← TRUE|>>   ELSE Index ← Index + 1 ENDIF|>> ENDWHILE",
      "Strengths and weaknesses|Works on unsorted data|Simple to write|Slow for long lists: may check every item"],
  a: "Carry out linear search with playing cards, counting comparisons. Then write it in pseudocode and run it with the 'Linear search' example in the runner. Support: complete the pseudocode with the comparison line missing. Stretch: change the search to count how many times the target appears.",
  w: [["What is a linear search?", "Checking each item in turn until the target is found or the list ends."], ["List: 15, 8, 23, 4, 42, 16. How many comparisons to find 4?", "4."], ["How many comparisons to show that 99 is not in that list?", "6."], ["Why does the loop condition include NOT Found?", "So the search stops as soon as the target is found."], ["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of linear search.", "Advantage: works on unsorted data and is simple. Disadvantage: slow for large lists."]],
  x: ["Does linear search need the data to be sorted?", "No."] },
{ t: "Lab: step through linear search with a trace table", o: "I can trace a linear search and use the trace table to explain it.",
  k: ["trace table: a table recording the value of each variable as an algorithm runs", "step: run one line at a time", "worst case: the situation needing the most work"],
  d: ["What is the worst case for a linear search of 100 items?", "The target is last or not there: 100 comparisons."],
  s: ["Step mode|Load 'Linear search' in the Pseudocode runner|Press Step to run one line at a time|Watch Index and Found change",
      "The trace table|Builds itself as the program runs|Compare it with one you complete by hand|Every change gets a row",
      "Investigate|Search for the first item, the last item and a missing item|Record the number of comparisons|Which case is best and which is worst?"],
  a: "Trace the linear search by hand for three targets, then step through the runner and compare its trace table with yours. Record comparisons for the best, worst and a missing-item case. Support: trace one target with a partner. Stretch: predict the number of comparisons for a list of 1000 items in each case.",
  w: [["What is the best case for a linear search?", "The target is the first item: one comparison."], ["What is the worst case?", "The target is last or missing: every item is checked."], ["List: 15, 8, 23, 4, 42, 16. Give the values of Index when searching for 23.", "1, 2, 3."], ["What does the Found column show when the search succeeds?", "It changes from FALSE to TRUE."], ["On average, about how many comparisons does a linear search of n items need when the target is present?", "About n/2."]],
  x: ["How many comparisons are needed in the worst case for 50 items?", "50."] },
{ t: "Bubble sort with cards", o: "I can carry out a bubble sort by hand.",
  k: ["bubble sort: a sort that compares neighbouring items and swaps them if they are in the wrong order", "pass: one run through the list", "swap: exchanging the positions of two items"],
  d: ["Put 5, 1, 4, 2, 8 in order. How did you do it?", "1, 2, 4, 5, 8. Any method; bubble sort is one."],
  s: ["The method|Compare items 1 and 2: swap if the first is bigger|Then 2 and 3, then 3 and 4, to the end of the list|That is one pass",
      "After each pass|The largest unsorted item has bubbled to the end|The next pass can stop one place earlier|Repeat until a pass makes no swaps",
      "Example: 5, 1, 4, 2, 8|Pass 1: 1, 4, 2, 5, 8 (three swaps)|Pass 2: 1, 2, 4, 5, 8 (one swap)|Pass 3: no swaps, so it is sorted"],
  a: "Sort three sets of eight cards by bubble sort, recording the list after each pass and counting comparisons and swaps. Then check with the Algorithm visualiser in CS Toolkit. Support: sort five cards. Stretch: find an order of five cards that needs the most passes, and explain why.",
  w: [["What is a pass?", "One run through the list, comparing neighbouring pairs."], ["Show the list 5, 1, 4, 2, 8 after the first pass.", "1, 4, 2, 5, 8."], ["How does bubble sort know when it has finished?", "A pass makes no swaps."], ["Why can each pass stop one place earlier than the last?", "The largest remaining item has already reached its final position at the end."], ["Explain why a list that is already sorted needs only one pass.", "The first pass finds no pairs to swap, so the algorithm stops."]],
  x: ["After the first pass, where is the largest item?", "At the end of the list."] },
{ t: "Bubble sort in pseudocode", o: "I can write a bubble sort and count its comparisons.",
  k: ["temporary variable: a variable used to hold one value during a swap", "flag: a Boolean that records whether something happened, such as a swap", "efficiency: how much work an algorithm needs"],
  d: ["Why do you need a third variable to swap two values?", "Copying one into the other would lose its old value. A temporary variable holds it."],
  s: ["The swap|>> Temp ← List[I]|>> List[I] ← List[I + 1]|>> List[I + 1] ← Temp",
      "The loops|REPEAT ... UNTIL NOT Swapped: keep making passes|FOR I ← 1 TO Last: compare neighbours|Last ← Last - 1 after each pass",
      "Counting comparisons|5, 1, 4, 2, 8: passes of 4, 3 and 2 comparisons, 9 in total|A sorted list of 8: one pass of 7 comparisons|A reversed list of 8: 7 + 6 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 28"],
  a: "Complete the bubble sort pseudocode, run it with the 'Bubble sort' example in the runner, then add a counter that outputs the number of comparisons. Test with sorted, reversed and random lists. Support: the swap lines are given. Stretch: change it to sort into descending order.",
  w: [["Write the three lines that swap List[I] and List[I + 1].", "Temp ← List[I]\nList[I] ← List[I + 1]\nList[I + 1] ← Temp"], ["What is the Swapped flag for?", "To record whether any swap happened in a pass, so the sort can stop when none did."], ["How many comparisons does this bubble sort make on 5, 1, 4, 2, 8?", "9."], ["How many comparisons for a reversed list of 8 items?", "28."], ["Explain why the number of comparisons depends on the starting order.", "The sort stops early when a pass makes no swaps, so nearly sorted data needs fewer passes."]],
  x: ["What would happen to a swap if the Temp line were left out?", "Both positions would end up holding the same value; one value would be lost."] },
{ t: "Lab: the bubble sort race", o: "I can measure how the starting order affects bubble sort.",
  k: ["best case: the starting order needing the least work", "worst case: the starting order needing the most work", "data set: a collection of values used for testing"],
  d: ["Predict: which needs more comparisons to bubble sort, a sorted list or a reversed list?", "The reversed list."],
  s: ["The race|Three lists of 8: sorted, reversed, random|Run each in the Algorithm visualiser|Record comparisons and swaps",
      "Then grow it|Try lists of 4, 8 and 12 items|Record the worst case each time|What happens to comparisons as the list doubles?",
      "What it shows|Best case: one pass|Worst case grows much faster than the list|Bubble sort is fine for small lists, slow for large ones"],
  a: "Run the race and complete the results table for sorted, reversed and random lists of 4, 8 and 12 items. Write three conclusions about bubble sort's efficiency. Support: complete the table for lists of 8. Stretch: plot worst-case comparisons against list length and describe the shape.",
  w: [["How many comparisons for a sorted list of 8?", "7."], ["How many comparisons for a reversed list of 8?", "28."], ["How many comparisons for a reversed list of 4?", "6."], ["What happened to the worst case when the list doubled from 4 to 8?", "It went from 6 to 28: more than four times as many."], ["Explain why bubble sort is not used for very large lists.", "The number of comparisons grows much faster than the length of the list, so it becomes very slow."]],
  x: ["Which starting order is the best case for bubble sort?", "Already sorted."] },
{ t: "Binary search and why sorted data matters", o: "I can explain how binary search works and compare it with linear search.",
  k: ["binary search: repeatedly checking the middle item of a sorted list and discarding the half that cannot contain the target", "sorted: in order", "midpoint: the middle position, (Low + High) DIV 2"],
  d: ["I am thinking of a number from 1 to 100. You may ask only 'higher or lower?'. What is your first guess, and why?", "50, the middle, to rule out half the numbers."],
  s: ["The method|Look at the middle item|If it is the target, stop|If the target is bigger, discard the lower half; if smaller, the upper half|Repeat on what is left",
      "Example: find 23 in 3, 8, 12, 19, 23, 31, 42|Mid 4: 19 is too small, Low becomes 5|Mid 6: 31 is too big, High becomes 5|Mid 5: 23 found after three comparisons",
      "Why it matters|Each comparison halves the list|1000 items need at most 10 comparisons|But the data must be sorted first"],
  a: "Play the higher-or-lower game, then carry out binary search by hand for three targets and compare with linear search. Run the 'Binary search' example in the runner and the Algorithm visualiser. Support: search a list of 7 items. Stretch: work out the maximum number of comparisons for 1 000 000 items.",
  w: [["What must be true of the data for binary search to work?", "It must be sorted."], ["Give the midpoints when searching for 23 in 3, 8, 12, 19, 23, 31, 42.", "4, 6, 5."], ["What is the maximum number of comparisons for 1000 items?", "10."], ["How many comparisons would a linear search need to find 42 in that list?", "7."], ["Explain why binary search is faster than linear search on large sorted lists.", "Each comparison removes half of the remaining items, so the number of comparisons grows very slowly as the list gets longer."]],
  x: ["Why can't binary search be used on an unsorted list?", "Discarding half depends on knowing that every item in that half is too big or too small."] },
{ t: "Trace tables by hand", o: "I can complete a trace table for an unfamiliar algorithm.",
  k: ["trace table: a table recording the value of each variable as an algorithm runs", "dry run: working through an algorithm by hand", "purpose: what an algorithm is for"],
  d: ["X ← 10, Y ← 3. Then X ← X - Y. What are X and Y?", "X is 7, Y is 3."],
  s: ["Set up|One column per variable, in the order they appear|An OUTPUT column|Write the input data where you can see it",
      "Work line by line|Write a new value only when a variable changes|Check every loop condition before each pass|Do not skip ahead",
      "Example|>> X ← 10|>> Y ← 3|>> WHILE X > Y|>>   X ← X - Y|>>   Y ← Y + 1|>> ENDWHILE|>> OUTPUT X, \" \", Y"],
  a: "Complete trace tables for the five algorithms on the sheet and state the purpose of each. Support: tables 1 and 2 have rows started. Stretch: trace the algorithm that uses an array and a nested IF.",
  w: [["Trace the example: give every value of X and Y and the output.", "X: 10, 7, 3. Y: 3, 4, 5. Output: 3 5."], ["Why does the WHILE loop in the example stop?", "X is 3 and Y is 5, so X > Y is false."], ["Trace: Total ← 0; FOR N ← 1 TO 4; IF N MOD 2 = 0 THEN Total ← Total + N. Give each value of Total.", "0, 2, 6."], ["What is a dry run?", "Working through an algorithm by hand."], ["Explain why you should check the loop condition before every pass, not only the first.", "The variables change inside the loop, so the condition can become false at any pass."]],
  x: ["When do you write a new value in a trace table?", "When a variable changes, or something is output."] },
{ t: "Lab: trace by hand, then check with the runner", o: "I can trace accurately and use the runner to check my work.",
  k: ["verify: to check that a result is correct", "discrepancy: a difference between two results", "exam technique: the habits that gain marks under test conditions"],
  d: ["What should you do if your trace table and the computer disagree?", "Find the first row that differs and recheck that line."],
  s: ["Method|Trace each algorithm by hand first, under timed conditions|Then run it in the Pseudocode runner|Compare your table with the automatic one",
      "Finding discrepancies|Find the first row that differs|Which line did you misread?|Write down the cause: loop boundary, MOD, comparison, order of lines",
      "Exam technique|Only write a value when it changes|Keep columns lined up|Write the output exactly as it would appear"],
  a: "Trace the six exam-style algorithms by hand in 30 minutes, then check each in the runner and record every discrepancy and its cause. Support: trace four algorithms. Stretch: write an algorithm with a subtle trap for a partner to trace.",
  w: [["What is a discrepancy?", "A difference between two results."], ["Name two common causes of trace table mistakes.", "Any two: wrong loop boundary, misreading MOD or DIV, wrong comparison, changing lines in the wrong order."], ["A REPEAT loop is traced as stopping one pass early. What was probably missed?", "The condition is tested after the pass, so the last pass still runs."], ["Trace: Count ← 0; REPEAT INPUT Num; Count ← Count + 1 UNTIL Num >= 1 AND Num <= 10, with inputs 0, 15, 7. Final Count?", "3."], ["Explain how checking with the runner improves your tracing.", "It shows exactly where your trace went wrong, so you learn which kind of mistake you make."]],
  x: ["What was your most common cause of discrepancy?", "A named cause."] },
{ t: "Test data, validation and verification", o: "I can choose test data and describe validation and verification checks.",
  k: ["validation: an automatic check that data is reasonable", "verification: a check that data has been entered or copied accurately", "test data: data chosen to check that a program works"],
  d: ["A program accepts whole numbers from 1 to 50. Give one number it should accept and one it should reject.", "For example 25 and 75."],
  s: ["Types of test data|Normal: should be accepted, such as 25|Abnormal: should be rejected, such as 75, -3 or 'abc'|Extreme: the largest and smallest accepted, 1 and 50|Boundary: either side of each limit, 0 and 1, 50 and 51",
      "Validation checks|Range: within limits|Length: number of characters|Type: whole number, letters only|Presence: not left empty|Format: matches a pattern|Check digit: a calculated digit that detects typing errors",
      "Verification|Double entry: type it twice and compare|Visual check: the user reads it back|Validation checks data is sensible; verification checks it was copied correctly"],
  a: "For each of the six inputs, choose one example of each type of test data with its expected result. Then name the best validation check for ten fields and say how each could be verified. Support: complete the test data for the first three. Stretch: write pseudocode for a range and type check using REPEAT ... UNTIL.",
  w: [["What is the difference between validation and verification?", "Validation checks data is reasonable. Verification checks it was entered or copied accurately."], ["For whole numbers from 1 to 50, give extreme data.", "1 and 50."], ["For whole numbers from 1 to 50, give boundary data.", "0 and 1, or 50 and 51 (one accepted and one rejected at a limit)."], ["Which validation check makes sure a field is not left empty?", "A presence check."], ["A user types their date of birth as 12/05/2010 instead of 21/05/2010. Explain why validation may not catch this, and how verification could.", "Both dates are valid, so a validation check accepts it. Double entry or a visual check would reveal the mistake."]],
  x: ["Name the four types of test data.", "Normal, abnormal, extreme, boundary."] },
{ t: "Assessed paper: exam-style questions", o: "I can answer exam-style questions on algorithms and pseudocode.",
  k: ["mark scheme: the list of points that earn marks", "command word: the word that says what to do, such as describe or explain", "concise: clear and without unnecessary words"],
  d: ["What does 'describe' ask for, and what does 'explain' ask for?", "Describe: say what happens. Explain: say why or how."],
  s: ["The paper|40 minutes, on your own|A trace table, a find-the-errors question, a write-the-algorithm question and a test data question|No runner, no notes",
      "Writing pseudocode for marks|Use the correct keywords in capitals|Close every IF, loop and CASE|Use meaningful variable names",
      "Find the errors|Read the purpose first|Trace with simple data|Give the line number and the corrected line"],
  a: "Complete the assessed paper under test conditions. Support: a keyword list is provided. Stretch: attempt the extension question on binary search.",
